---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Shot from "../../components/Shot.astro";
import P from "../../components/P.astro";
import Gallery from "../../components/Gallery.astro";
import Demo from "../../components/Demo.astro";
import Phone from "../../components/Phone.astro";

const S = "portal";
const people = [
  { id: "des", label: "Designer", x: 10, y: 22, ax: 8, ay: 50 },
  { id: "am", label: "Account manager", x: 46, y: 10, ax: 30, ay: 50 },
  { id: "head", label: "Design head", x: 84, y: 24, ax: 52, ay: 50 },
  { id: "buyer", label: "Retailer buyer", x: 18, y: 80, ax: 74, ay: 50 },
  { id: "lic", label: "Licensor", x: 74, y: 84, ax: 94, ay: 50 },
];
const messy = [
  ["des", "am", "WhatsApp"], ["am", "head", "Phone"], ["am", "buyer", "Email"], ["buyer", "des", "Email"],
  ["head", "des", "In person"], ["buyer", "lic", "Email"], ["lic", "am", "Spreadsheet"], ["des", "lic", "Phone"],
];
const pos = Object.fromEntries(people.map(p => [p.id, p]));
const stages = [
  { name: "To Do", note: "Brief arrives" },
  { name: "In Progress", note: "The one manual move" },
  { name: "In Selection", note: "Board shared with the retailer" },
  { name: "Complete", note: "Selection locked, or every design rejected" },
];
const lane = ["Awaiting CADs", "PLM pending", "Submitted", "Approved"];
// Phone screens, captured at 390 × 844, in the app's navigation order (same grouping as the desktop gallery)
const phoneGroups = [
  { title: "Sign in and access", short: "Sign in", ids: ["m-login", "m-redirecting", "m-session-error", "m-offline", "m-access-refused"] },
  { title: "Menu 1 · Project Board", short: "Project Board", ids: ["m-board-table", "m-board-kanban", "m-board-gantt"] },
  { title: "Project Board › Inside a brief", short: "Inside a brief", ids: ["m-brief", "m-brief-approved", "m-brief-partial", "m-brief-rejected", "m-brief-resubmitted"] },
  { title: "Menu 2 · Team Capacity", short: "Team Capacity", ids: ["m-capacity"] },
  { title: "Menu 3 · Design Library", short: "Design Library", ids: ["m-library", "m-item-detail"] },
  { title: "Menu 4 · Trends", short: "Trends", ids: ["m-trends", "m-trends-filters", "m-trends-detail", "m-trends-boards", "m-trends-board-new", "m-trends-board", "m-trends-board-empty", "m-trends-add", "m-trends-uploads", "m-upload", "m-upload-extract", "m-upload-review", "m-upload-details", "m-upload-done"] },
  { title: "Menu 5 · Visualisation", short: "Visualisation", ids: ["m-visualisation"] },
  { title: "Menu 6 · Apparel Blanks", short: "Apparel Blanks", ids: ["m-blanks"] },
  { title: "Menu 7 · Insights", short: "Insights", ids: ["m-insights", "m-coming-soon"] },
  { title: "Menu 8 · Settings", short: "Settings", ids: ["m-settings"] },
  { title: "Account menu · Notifications", short: "Notifications", ids: ["m-notifications"] },
  { title: "Retailer portal › Received projects", short: "Retailer projects", ids: ["m-r-home", "m-r-library"] },
  { title: "Retailer portal › Responses and notifications", short: "Retailer responses", ids: ["m-r-responses", "m-r-notifications"] },
];
// Voice-to-brief failure states as the phone bottom sheet (vsp-*), same groups as the desktop drawer captures (vs-*)
const voicePhoneGroups = [
  { title: "A · Starting and the microphone", short: "A · Starting", ids: ["A1", "A2", "A3", "A4", "A5", "A6", "A7"] },
  { title: "B · While recording", short: "B · Recording", ids: ["B1", "B2", "B3", "B4", "B5", "B6", "B7", "B8"] },
  { title: "C · Connection and service", short: "C · Connection", ids: ["C1", "C2", "C3", "C4", "C5", "C6", "C7"] },
  { title: "D · Leaving and coming back", short: "D · Leaving", ids: ["D1", "D2", "D3", "D4"] },
  { title: "E · Understanding what was said", short: "E · Understanding", ids: ["E1", "E2", "E3", "E4", "E5", "E6", "E7", "E8", "E9"] },
  { title: "F · Review and access", short: "F · Review", ids: ["F1", "F2"] },
].map(g => ({ ...g, ids: g.ids.map(k => `vsp-${k}`) }));
// The sheet sits over the dimmed app, so the status bar takes the scrim colour
const voiceBars = Object.fromEntries(voicePhoneGroups.flatMap(g => g.ids).map(id => [id, "#92949B"]));
const phoneCount = phoneGroups.reduce((n, g) => n + g.ids.length, 0);
// Status bar colour = the top row of each screen, so the bar reads as part of the app
const phoneBars = {
  "m-login": "#F9F9FB", "m-redirecting": "#F9F9FB", "m-session-error": "#F9F9FB", "m-offline": "#F9F9FB", "m-trends-filters": "#F9F9FB",
  "m-trends-add": "#FFFFFF", "m-trends-board-new": "#FFFFFF", "m-trends-detail": "#FFFFFF", "m-upload-details": "#92949C",
};
---
<Base title="Design approvals portal · Ankur Singh" description="Case study: replacing a design-approval lifecycle run over WhatsApp, email and phone with one portal and a design system built from scratch.">
  <article>
    <header class="hero wrap">
      <p class="mono reveal"><a href="/">Work</a> · B2B platform · Design system · Live</p>
      <h1 class="reveal">Design-approval portal</h1>
      <p class="lede reveal">
        My client, <P slug={S} field="client" />, ran its whole design-approval lifecycle over phone calls, WhatsApp, email and conversations.
        I designed the portal that replaced it: 64 screens across two connected portals, built on a design system I created from scratch.
      </p>
      <dl class="meta reveal">
        <div><dt class="mono">Client</dt><dd><P slug={S} field="client" /></dd></div>
        <div><dt class="mono">My role</dt><dd>Product designer embedded with the client: research, design system, every screen and its spec</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="mono">Timeline</dt><dd>~3 months of design · live 6+ months</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="mono">Outcome</dt><dd>About 1,000 users · workflow email from 1,000+ a day to zero · 300%+ productivity, measured by the client</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </header>

    <div class="wrap reveal">
      <Shot slug={S} id="board" eager caption="Project Board: every live brief, by department. Tap the numbers for notes."
        spots={[
          { x: 9, y: 26, title: "One tree for the whole business", text: "Department, then retailer, then brief. A design head sees everything; a designer filters to their own." },
          { x: 58, y: 13, title: "Three views of the same briefs", text: "Table, Kanban and timeline, with nothing re-entered between them." },
          { x: 66, y: 49, title: "Deadlines that warn you", text: "Overdue and due-soon dates carry colour and an icon, never colour alone." },
        ]} />
    </div>

    <section class="wrap sec grid12">
      <div class="h reveal"><p class="mono">The problem</p><h2>Approvals ran on phone, WhatsApp and email</h2></div>
      <div class="b reveal">
        <p>A single licensed-apparel brief moves through designers, account managers, design heads, retailer buyers and the licensor. Each hand-off happened in a different channel: the global design head alone wrote, replied to or forwarded about 500 emails a day, and the calls and WhatsApp messages were never recorded anywhere. So nobody could answer three basic questions: <em>where is this brief, who is waiting on whom, and who has too much on?</em></p>
        <p class="muted small">Diagram reconstructed from my interviews, for this case study.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap">
      <figure class="flow reveal" data-state="before">
        <div class="flow-bar">
          <span class="mono">How a brief travelled</span>
          <div class="seg" role="group" aria-label="Before or after the portal">
            <button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-set="before">Before</button>
            <button type="button" aria-pressed="false" data-set="after">With the portal</button>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="stage">
          <svg class="lines before" viewBox="0 0 100 100" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true">
            {messy.map(([a, b]) => <line x1={pos[a].x} y1={pos[a].y} x2={pos[b].x} y2={pos[b].y} />)}
          </svg>
          <svg class="lines after" viewBox="0 0 100 100" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true">
            <line x1="8" y1="50" x2="94" y2="50" />
          </svg>
          {messy.map(([a, b, ch]) => (
            <span class="chan mono" style={`left:${(pos[a].x + pos[b].x) / 2}%;top:${(pos[a].y + pos[b].y) / 2}%`}>{ch}</span>
          ))}
          <span class="portal mono">One portal: brief → board → selection → approval</span>
          {people.map(p => (
            <span class="node" style={`--bx:${p.x}%;--by:${p.y}%;--ax:${p.ax}%;--ay:${p.ay}%`}>{p.label}</span>
          ))}
        </div>
        <figcaption class="sr">Before: five roles connected by eight different channels. With the portal: the same five roles on one line.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap sec grid12">
      <div class="h reveal"><p class="mono">Research</p><h2>Interviews and review rounds</h2></div>
      <div class="b reveal">
        <p>I interviewed about ten people: fashion designers, apparel designers, department heads, the global head of design and retailer buyers. Then I went back to real users in several rounds while designing.</p>
        <p>Each round ended with a design presentation to the client's head of design, who signed it off. I checked every pattern with the development architect, so what I designed could be built.</p>
        <ul class="ticks">
          <li>One place to see every brief, whatever its stage</li>
          <li>The retailer answers inside the same system, not by email</li>
          <li>Status should change itself; people shouldn't have to remember to</li>
          <li>See overload before it becomes a missed deadline</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="muted small">What the interviews pointed to, and what the design is built around.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap sec">
      <div class="grid12">
        <div class="h reveal"><p class="mono">Decision</p><h2>Brief statuses</h2></div>
        <div class="b reveal">
          <p>A brief has four statuses everywhere: To Do, In Progress, In Selection, Complete. People make only one move by hand, To Do to In Progress, and only once the brief has a designer, a start date and the expected boards. Everything after that is set by what actually happens: sharing a board, the retailer locking a selection.</p>
          <p>Licensor approval runs on its own lane, design by design, and never sends a brief backwards.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="life reveal" aria-label="Brief lifecycle">
        <ol class="stages">
          {stages.map((s, i) => <li style={`--i:${i}`}><b>{s.name}</b><span class="mono">{s.note}</span></li>)}
        </ol>
        <div class="lane"><span class="mono">Per design, after selection</span><ol>{lane.map((l, i) => <li style={`--i:${i + 4}`}>{l}</li>)}</ol></div>
      </div>
      <div class="pair2 reveal">
        <Shot slug={S} id="brief" caption="The brief page: overview left, the work right"
          spots={[
            { x: 64, y: 47, title: "Two routes to a board", text: "Upload a finished PDF, or build one from the library. Both end in the same selection flow." },
            { x: 20, y: 62, title: "Complexity vs time available", text: "Captured when the brief is created, so a tight brief is visible before work starts." },
            { x: 52, y: 80, title: "References stay references", text: "Trends and library picks give context but never land on the board by accident." },
          ]} />
        <Shot slug={S} id="board-ready" caption="Board ready to share with the retailer" />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="focus" aria-labelledby="voice-title">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="grid12">
          <div class="h reveal"><p class="mono kick">Focus · Voice to brief</p><h2 id="voice-title">Voice to brief</h2></div>
          <div class="b reveal">
            <p>Briefs often start as a conversation: a call with a buyer, a quick chat after a meeting. Typing nine fields from memory afterwards is where details get lost. So I designed a way to speak the brief instead. You talk the way you would to a colleague, the form fills itself in as you go, and the portal asks about whatever you left out.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="vdemo reveal">
          <Demo slug={S} id="voice-demo" caption="The working prototype, recorded end to end: tap the mic, talk, answer what's missing, review, create." />
        </div>

        <div class="grid12 vblock">
          <div class="h reveal"><p class="mono">Decision</p><h3>Where voice starts</h3></div>
          <div class="b reveal">
            <ul class="opts">
              <li><b>1a · Mic inside the form</b><span class="tag">Chosen</span><p>New brief opens the usual drawer, and the first thing in it is "Speak it instead". Nobody has to learn a new entry point, and people who prefer the form never meet an extra step.</p></li>
              <li><b>1b · Choose how to start</b><p>Voice gets equal billing, but everyone pays one extra tap, every time, even for the path they always pick.</p></li>
              <li><b>1c · Split button</b><p>A mic beside New brief starts recording straight away, which suits someone walking out of a call. It can be added on top of 1a later.</p></li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="reveal vimg"><Shot slug={S} id="v-entry" caption="The three explored entry points" /></div>

        <div class="grid12 vblock">
          <div class="h reveal"><p class="mono">The flow</p><h3>Listen, ask, review, create</h3></div>
          <div class="b reveal"><p>Step through it. Every screen here is from the working prototype, recorded while playing a sample call.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="vflow reveal">
          <div class="seg dark" role="tablist" aria-label="Voice flow step">
            <button type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="true" data-vf="v-ready">1 · Start</button>
            <button type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-vf="v-listen">2 · Listen</button>
            <button type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-vf="v-ask">3 · Ask</button>
            <button type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-vf="v-review">4 · Review</button>
          </div>
          <div class="vstack2">
            <div class="vf on" data-for="v-ready"><Shot slug={S} id="v-ready" caption="Tap and talk. Typing is always one tap away." /></div>
            <div class="vf" data-for="v-listen"><Shot slug={S} id="v-listen" caption="What it understood is highlighted, and the brief fills in live"
              spots={[
                { x: 74, y: 20, title: "Highlighted as it's heard", text: "Every word it turned into a field is marked, so you can see what it understood." },
                { x: 74, y: 52, title: "The form fills itself in", text: "Fields complete as you talk: 6 of 9 after a few seconds." },
              ]} /></div>
            <div class="vf" data-for="v-ask"><Shot slug={S} id="v-ask" caption="One question per gap, answer by voice or skip"
              spots={[{ x: 74, y: 14, title: "Asked once", text: "Each missing field gets one spoken question. Skip it, and it's flagged instead of asked again." }]} /></div>
            <div class="vf" data-for="v-review"><Shot slug={S} id="v-review" caption="Heard vs inferred, and nothing skipped slips through"
              spots={[
                { x: 92, y: 8, title: "Heard vs inferred", text: "Anything the portal worked out for itself, like a full name from 'Amira', gets a dashed Inferred tag." },
                { x: 80, y: 51, title: "Needed, not forgotten", text: "A skipped required field shows as Needed, and Create stays locked until it's filled." },
                { x: 74, y: 70, title: "Designs from the conversation", text: "'Six pyjama sets, eight nightdresses' becomes placeholder design rows, ready to assign." },
              ]} /></div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="grid12 vblock">
          <div class="h reveal"><p class="mono">Principles</p><h3>Rules for the voice feature</h3></div>
          <div class="b reveal">
            <ul class="vrules">
              <li><b>Asked once, then flagged</b><p>No interrogation. One question per gap; skipped answers become a visible to-do.</p></li>
              <li><b>Heard vs inferred</b><p>What you said shows plainly. What the portal guessed is tagged, with the reason on hover.</p></li>
              <li><b>Typing is one tap away</b><p>Voice is an option, never a trap. "Type it instead" sits in every state.</p></li>
              <li><b>The recording stays</b><p>The audio and transcript are saved on the brief, so anyone can check what was actually said.</p></li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="vphone reveal">
          <Shot slug={S} id="v-phone" class="phone" caption="On a phone: the same flow as a bottom sheet, primary action under the thumb" />
          <div>
            <p class="mono">Edge cases</p>
            <h3 class="big3">37 failure states</h3>
            <p>Voice fails in ways forms don't: a blocked microphone, a headset unplugged mid-sentence, a laptop that sleeps, a dropped connection after "I'm done", two people with the same first name, a licence that has expired. I mapped every failure from the test list into six groups and designed what the user sees for each, so a broken moment always ends with a clear next step and nothing said is lost.</p>
            <p class="muted">Open any state below to see it full size.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="vdev">
          <div class="seg dark" role="tablist" aria-label="Show the states on">
            <button type="button" role="tab" id="vd-tab-phone" aria-controls="vd-phone" aria-selected="true" data-vd="phone">Phone</button>
            <button type="button" role="tab" id="vd-tab-desk" aria-controls="vd-desk" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1" data-vd="desk">Desktop</button>
          </div>
          <div class="vd" id="vd-phone" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="vd-tab-phone">
            <Gallery slug={S} phone label="Voice states on a phone" bars={voiceBars} groups={voicePhoneGroups} />
          </div>
          <div class="vd" id="vd-desk" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="vd-tab-desk" hidden>
            <Gallery slug={S} tall groups={[
            { title: "A · Starting and the microphone", ids: ["vs-A1", "vs-A2", "vs-A3", "vs-A4", "vs-A5", "vs-A6", "vs-A7"] },
            { title: "B · While recording", ids: ["vs-B1", "vs-B2", "vs-B3", "vs-B4", "vs-B5", "vs-B6", "vs-B7", "vs-B8"] },
            { title: "C · Connection and service", ids: ["vs-C1", "vs-C2", "vs-C3", "vs-C4", "vs-C5", "vs-C6", "vs-C7"] },
            { title: "D · Leaving and coming back", ids: ["vs-D1", "vs-D2", "vs-D3", "vs-D4"] },
            { title: "E · Understanding what was said", ids: ["vs-E1", "vs-E2", "vs-E3", "vs-E4", "vs-E5", "vs-E6", "vs-E7", "vs-E8", "vs-E9"] },
            { title: "F · Review and access", ids: ["vs-F1", "vs-F2"] },
            ]} />
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap sec">
      <div class="grid12">
        <div class="h reveal"><p class="mono">Decision</p><h2>Table, Kanban and timeline</h2></div>
        <div class="b reveal"><p>Design heads plan in timelines, designers work in columns, account managers scan tables. Rather than pick one, the same briefs show three ways, with filters that follow you between them.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="views reveal" data-view="board">
        <div class="seg" role="tablist" aria-label="Project Board view">
          <button type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="true" data-v="board">Table</button>
          <button type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-v="kanban">Kanban</button>
          <button type="button" role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-v="gantt">Timeline</button>
        </div>
        <div class="vstack">
          <div class="v" data-for="kanban"><Shot slug={S} id="kanban" /></div>
          <div class="v" data-for="gantt"><Shot slug={S} id="gantt" /></div>
          <div class="v on" data-for="board"><Shot slug={S} id="board" /></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap sec">
      <div class="grid12">
        <div class="h reveal"><p class="mono">Decision</p><h2>The retailer portal</h2></div>
        <div class="b reveal">
          <p>The biggest leak in the old process was the retailer's answer: it came back as an email, a call or a meeting. So I designed a second, connected portal for buyers. They approve everything in one tap, make a partial selection with comments on individual designs, or reject with required feedback.</p>
          <p>On the supplier side, a partial answer lets the designer lock what was approved and revise the rest at the same time, instead of waiting for one before starting the other.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pair2 reveal">
        <Shot slug={S} id="retailer-review" caption="The buyer's side"
          spots={[
            { x: 80, y: 27, title: "Three ways to answer", text: "Approve all, make a selection, or reject. Feedback is only required for a rejection." },
            { x: 18, y: 27, title: "Read-only context", text: "The buyer sees the brief they asked for, never the supplier's internal workflow." },
          ]} />
        <Shot slug={S} id="partial" caption="The designer's side: a partial approval"
          spots={[
            { x: 64, y: 33, title: "The answer, in one line", text: "Who responded, when, and how many designs need changes." },
            { x: 59, y: 66, title: "Comments stay on the design", text: "Feedback is pinned under the tile it's about, not lost in a thread." },
            { x: 80, y: 96, title: "Two tracks at once", text: "Revise and resubmit the three, or proceed with the seven, or both." },
          ]} />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap sec">
      <div class="grid12">
        <div class="h reveal"><p class="mono">Iteration</p><h2>Team Capacity</h2></div>
        <div class="b reveal">
          <p>The first version had six equal KPI cards, and they answered nothing. I reframed the page around one question, <em>who is overloaded this week, and how do we fix it</em>, and explored three directions for the top of the page: exceptions first, three action cards, or a sentence with two lists.</p>
          <p>Three action cards won. Hours lead and percentages follow. Each designer's bar carries a <strong>peak-week marker</strong>, because 60/60/190/60 averages to about 92% and reads as healthy when one week is nearly double. The page never changes the board by itself: it proposes a move, and the brief owner approves it.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="reveal"><Shot slug={S} id="capacity-options" caption="Explored: three directions built on the same decisions" /></div>
      <div class="reveal spaced">
        <Shot slug={S} id="capacity" caption="Shipped: 80 designers, eight weeks, three actions"
          spots={[
            { x: 23, y: 28, title: "Act, don't just count", text: "Over capacity, spare to give, delivery at risk, each with its next step." },
            { x: 31, y: 53, title: "Average plus peak", text: "The bar fills to the average; the red tick marks the worst week." },
            { x: 88, y: 18, title: "One page, three lenses", text: "Preview it as a designer, an account manager or a design head." },
          ]} />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap sec">
      <div class="grid12">
        <div class="h reveal"><p class="mono">AI</p><h2>Trend extraction with review</h2></div>
        <div class="b reveal">
          <p>Trend reports arrive as long PDFs. The portal reads them page by page and pulls out every image with a suggested title, tags and category. Designers then confirm in batches, while a focus mode walks them through only the low-confidence ones. Duplicates start excluded. Nothing is published until a person says so.</p>
          <p>Every AI feature can be switched off by an admin, with a monthly query limit and a budget alert. And in visualisation, the rule is written into the screen: <em>the artwork itself is never generated, only the world around it.</em></p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pair2 reveal">
        <Shot slug={S} id="trend-review" caption="Batch review with confidence scores"
          spots={[
            { x: 22, y: 33, title: "Filter by doubt", text: "Low confidence and duplicates are counted and one tap away." },
            { x: 76, y: 25, title: "Focus mode", text: "Walks through just the images the model wasn't sure about." },
          ]} />
        <Shot slug={S} id="ai-config" caption="Admin controls and budget for every AI feature" />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap sec">
      <div class="grid12">
        <div class="h reveal"><p class="mono">System</p><h2>The design system</h2></div>
        <div class="b reveal">
          <p>I wrote the system before the screens: one brand yellow held back for actions, cool slate neutrals, Poppins, a 4px grid, pill-shaped controls and a single easing curve. Components only use semantic tokens, so re-theming is a token change. That's how dark mode arrived later as a remap, not a rewrite.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <ul class="rules reveal">
        <li class="card"><span class="mono">Decision</span><h3>Warning is burnt orange, never yellow</h3><p>Yellow means "do this". A yellow warning would read as a button.</p></li>
        <li class="card"><span class="mono">Decision</span><h3>Focus ring is slate, not yellow</h3><p>Yellow on white fails the 3:1 contrast a focus indicator needs (WCAG 2.4.11).</p></li>
        <li class="card"><span class="mono">Decision</span><h3>Colour is never the only signal</h3><p>Every status badge carries an icon or a word. Touch targets are at least 44px.</p></li>
        <li class="card"><span class="mono">Decision</span><h3>Drawers for work, dialogs for decisions</h3><p>Forms and reviews open in a drawer. A dialog only ever asks one yes/no question, and never stacks on another.</p></li>
      </ul>
